systems.doc:
Yield lets other goroutines run, as runtime.Gosched does. Use it where code waits for another goroutine without any World operation in between, such as a loop spinning on an atomic:

for flag.Load() == 0 { w.Yield() }

In a simulated World only one goroutine runs at a time, so such a loop would otherwise never let the other one run. There Yield returns only after another goroutine has taken a step or a timer has fired: the goroutine waited for runs, time advances while it sleeps, and a spin that nothing can ever end is reported as a deadlock.

